---
// Persona page for Mene Demestihas (per Notion: "About Mene (personal) — page spec").
// Narrative copy delivered by VPstrategy.agent handoff 2026-06-06 (GREEN-tier, QA-passed).
// Sections are inlined rather than split into src/components/mene/* — same structure, less
// fragmentation for prose-only sections. Connect CTA reuses the shared LeadFormModal
// (data-lead-intent="mene"). Copy is intentionally em-dash-free (voice choice, do not "fix").
//
// OPEN (per spec): hero-quality 1600x1000 headshot + a section-3 contextual photo still needed
// (using the existing square avatar as a stand-in); custom OG image; Mene's personal LinkedIn URL
// for Person.sameAs (company page used as a placeholder below).
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const linkedin = 'https://www.linkedin.com/company/metrias-medical/';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Mene Demestihas — Founder of Metrias Medical | Metrias Medical"
  description="Physician, operator, founder of Metrias Medical. The story of why Metrias exists and what we're solving in hospital discharge coordination."
>
  {/* 1 — Hero (muted; not Grainient, which is reserved for home) */}
  <section class="bg-provider-blue-dark">
    <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-7xl items-center gap-10 px-6 py-20 lg:grid-cols-[1.4fr_1fr] lg:gap-16 lg:px-8 lg:py-28">
      <div>
        <p class="font-body text-base font-semibold text-ai-gold">Physician, operator, founder of Metrias Medical</p>
        <h1 class="mt-4 font-heading text-[2.5rem] leading-tight font-bold text-white md:text-[3.25rem]">
          Mene Demestihas, MD
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-6 max-w-xl font-body text-lg leading-relaxed text-white/80">
          I left the bedside to fix the part of discharge no one would claim. This is the short
          version of why.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-col items-start gap-4 sm:flex-row sm:items-center">
          <button
            type="button"
            data-lead-trigger
            data-lead-intent="mene"
            class="rounded-lg bg-ai-gold px-8 py-3 font-body text-base font-medium text-provider-blue transition-colors hover:bg-ai-gold/90 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-white"
          >
            Stay in touch
          </button>
          <a
            href="/about"
            class="font-body text-base font-medium text-white underline underline-offset-4 transition-colors hover:text-white/80"
          >
            More about Metrias &rarr;
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="order-first overflow-hidden rounded-2xl lg:order-none">
        <img
          src="/images/team/mene-avatar.png"
          alt="Mene Demestihas, MD — Founder & CEO of Metrias Medical"
          width="600"
          height="600"
          class="h-auto w-full object-cover"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 2 — The clinical question */}
  <section class="bg-white py-20 md:py-28">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="font-heading text-[2rem] leading-tight font-bold text-black md:text-[2.25rem]">
        The clinical question that didn't go away
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 font-body text-lg leading-relaxed text-payer-slate">
        <p>
          I would walk into a room and find someone dressed, bag packed, sitting on the edge of a
          made bed. Medically, they were done. There was no order left to write. They were ready to
          go home, and they were still there the next morning, and the morning after that. Not for
          any reason I could treat. Because someone hadn't finished the phone calls.
        </p>
        <p>
          The hold was always administrative. A skilled nursing facility that hadn't called back. A
          SNF that reviewed the chart and said no. A prior auth still pending. An insurance approval
          that never came through. Transport nobody had booked. The patient was ready and the system
          around them was not, and the gap had nothing to do with medicine. It was coordination work
          that simply hadn't been done by anyone.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 3 — The transition */}
  <section class="bg-off-white py-20 md:py-28">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="font-heading text-[2rem] leading-tight font-bold text-black md:text-[2.25rem]">
        From the bedside to the systems above it
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 font-body text-lg leading-relaxed text-payer-slate">
        <p>
          What made it a clinical problem, not just something I carried home, was the bed itself. A
          bed held by a patient who is ready to leave is a bed the next person can't have. The delay
          doesn't only sit on the person in the room. It lands on the patient downstairs, waiting on
          a stretcher for the bed that won't open. I was watching one person's discharge friction
          become another person's delayed care.
        </p>
        <p>
          When I moved into operator roles, I expected to find a team that needed fixing. Instead I
          found the same pattern on every unit and across the whole system. Not one slow floor. A
          structural gap, work everyone quietly assumed belonged to someone else. Nationally,
          case-manager vacancies run around twenty percent, and the people still in the seats spend
          much of the day on work that never needed a clinical license. You can't make an empty seat
          more efficient. The work just isn't getting done.
        </p>
        <p>
          I didn't go looking for a problem to build a company around. I lived this one, from inside
          the room. So I left the bedside to fix the part of it no one would claim.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 4 — Why Metrias */}
  <section class="bg-white py-20 md:py-28">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="font-heading text-[2rem] leading-tight font-bold text-black md:text-[2.25rem]">
        We built the role that was missing
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 font-body text-lg leading-relaxed text-payer-slate">
        <p>
          So I built what was missing at the bedside: a person to actually do the work. Metrias puts
          a dedicated, remote, non-clinical discharge specialist onto the care team, an expeditor
          whose only job is the coordination that holds patients in beds. The phone tag with SNFs,
          the rejections, the prior auths, the transport, the insurance holds. Not a tool that tells
          already-stretched staff what to do next. A person who picks up the calls and finishes
          them, and who stays out of the clinical staff's way on purpose. That's the role I'd have
          wanted on my floor.
        </p>
        <p>
          While the expeditor works, the software quietly records what actually happens: every call
          placed, every SNF that says no, and where each hold lands. You get a real, structured
          picture of where patients actually get stuck, not just what eventually got typed into a
          chart. Most tools read what already got written down and tell staff what to do. We do the
          work and measure the friction itself: data the EHR can't see.
        </p>
        <p>
          The work comes first. The data is what the work leaves behind, and over time it becomes
          the thing the EHR never could, a map of where care actually stalls. That map is what
          eventually tells us what to automate, because we measured it instead of guessing. The
          person is the wedge. Everything after that follows from the work getting done.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 5 — What I'm working on now */}
  <section class="bg-off-white py-20 md:py-28">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="font-heading text-[2rem] leading-tight font-bold text-black md:text-[2.25rem]">
        What I'm working on right now
      </h2>
      <ul class="mt-6 space-y-4">
        <li class="flex gap-3 font-body text-lg leading-relaxed text-payer-slate">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-ai-gold" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span>
            Building the expeditor role around the calls care teams actually dread: the hard,
            unglamorous coordination work, not the parts that are easy to put in a pitch.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 font-body text-lg leading-relaxed text-payer-slate">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-ai-gold" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span>
            Turning the captured friction into a clear read on where patients actually get held up,
            the picture a hospital can't pull from its EHR.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 font-body text-lg leading-relaxed text-payer-slate">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-ai-gold" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span>
            Keeping the build honest to the bedside. The deliverable is the finished work, not
            another piece of software that points at the problem and tells staff what to do. Out of
            clinical staff's way, by design.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 6 — How to reach me */}
  <section class="bg-provider-blue-dark py-20 md:py-28">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 text-center lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="font-heading text-[2rem] leading-tight font-bold text-white md:text-[2.25rem]">
        Reach me directly
      </h2>
      <p class="mx-auto mt-4 max-w-xl font-body text-lg text-white/80">
        Scanned my card, or found me on LinkedIn? Leave a note and I'll get back to you.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-col items-center justify-center gap-4 sm:flex-row">
        <button
          type="button"
          data-lead-trigger
          data-lead-intent="mene"
          class="rounded-lg bg-ai-gold px-8 py-3 font-body text-base font-medium text-provider-blue transition-colors hover:bg-ai-gold/90 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-white"
        >
          Stay in touch
        </button>
        <a
          href={linkedin}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="rounded-lg border-2 border-white/80 px-8 py-3 font-body text-base font-medium text-white transition-colors hover:bg-white/10 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-white"
        >
          Connect on LinkedIn
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script
    is:inline
    type="application/ld+json"
    set:html={JSON.stringify({
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'Person',
      name: 'Mene Demestihas',
      jobTitle: 'Founder & CEO',
      worksFor: {
        '@type': 'Organization',
        name: 'Metrias Medical',
        url: 'https://www.metriasmedical.com',
      },
      sameAs: ['https://www.linkedin.com/company/metrias-medical/'],
    })}
  />

  {/* PostHog: mene_page_cta_clicked (form open). Successful submits are captured server-side by the
      lead Worker as lead_captured with source_slug='mene'. */}
  <script is:inline>
    document.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
      const t = e.target;
      if (t && t.closest && t.closest('[data-lead-trigger]') && window.posthog) {
        window.posthog.capture('mene_page_cta_clicked');
      }
    });
  </script>
</BaseLayout>
